REV.page({"course":"english2","id":"w05","title":"Mood and modal verbs","summary":"Modal auxiliaries (can, could, may, might, must, shall, should, will, would) add the speaker's view of a situation, such as possibility, ability, permission or obligation, without marking tense. This week explains what they are and how to choose between can and could, will and would, could and would, must, shall and should, and may and might.","n":5,"lectures":[{"code":"5.1","title":"Mood & modal verbs | expressing ability, obligation, permission & possibility","video":"zpXHE-M5nco","seconds":1242},{"code":"5.2","title":"Modal verbs: can or could | usage, differences & examples for clear communication","video":"qglDbdAnzkY","seconds":985},{"code":"5.3","title":"Will vs would | usage, differences & examples for accurate communication","video":"bLYW1Xqe2Cg","seconds":822},{"code":"5.4","title":"Could vs would | usage, differences & examples for confident communication","video":"meJgUe5W6Yg","seconds":801},{"code":"5.5","title":"Modals (auxiliaries) : must, shall & should | usage, differences & examples","video":"kRKZiO9QyZg","seconds":1322},{"code":"5.6","title":"Modal verbs: may & might | usage, differences & examples for accurate communication","video":"-PACFxfrcgc","seconds":1218}],"toc":[{"id":"at-a-glance","level":2,"title":"At a glance"},{"id":"modal-auxiliaries-and-mood","level":2,"title":"Modal auxiliaries and mood"},{"id":"two-sets-of-modals","level":3,"title":"Two sets of modals"},{"id":"modality-and-mood","level":3,"title":"Modality and mood"},{"id":"the-readings-of-a-modal","level":3,"title":"The readings of a modal"},{"id":"how-a-modal-behaves-in-a-sentence","level":3,"title":"How a modal behaves in a sentence"},{"id":"can-and-could","level":2,"title":"Can and could"},{"id":"ability","level":3,"title":"Ability"},{"id":"possibility","level":3,"title":"Possibility"},{"id":"polite-expressions","level":3,"title":"Polite expressions"},{"id":"the-lecture-s-activity","level":3,"title":"The lecture’s activity"},{"id":"will-and-would","level":2,"title":"Will and would"},{"id":"five-uses-of-will","level":3,"title":"Five uses of will"},{"id":"would-for-the-past","level":3,"title":"Would for the past"},{"id":"conditionals","level":3,"title":"Conditionals"},{"id":"requests-and-proposals","level":3,"title":"Requests and proposals"},{"id":"reported-speech-and-past-habits","level":3,"title":"Reported speech and past habits"},{"id":"more-uses-of-would","level":3,"title":"More uses of would"},{"id":"could-and-would","level":2,"title":"Could and would"},{"id":"both-can-talk-about-the-past","level":3,"title":"Both can talk about the past"},{"id":"possible-or-imaginary","level":3,"title":"Possible or imaginary?"},{"id":"polite-expressions-2","level":3,"title":"Polite expressions"},{"id":"the-lecture-s-activity-2","level":3,"title":"The lecture’s activity"},{"id":"must-shall-and-should","level":2,"title":"Must, shall and should"},{"id":"must-for-necessity","level":3,"title":"Must for necessity"},{"id":"must-for-obligation","level":3,"title":"Must for obligation"},{"id":"mustn-t","level":3,"title":"Mustn’t"},{"id":"shall","level":3,"title":"Shall"},{"id":"shall-for-strong-obligation","level":3,"title":"Shall for strong obligation"},{"id":"should","level":3,"title":"Should"},{"id":"may-and-might","level":2,"title":"May and might"},{"id":"may-for-possibility","level":3,"title":"May for possibility"},{"id":"may-for-permission","level":3,"title":"May for permission"},{"id":"may-for-concession","level":3,"title":"May for concession"},{"id":"might","level":3,"title":"Might"},{"id":"summary","level":2,"title":"Summary"},{"id":"check-yourself","level":2,"title":"Check yourself"}],"html":"<h2 id=\"at-a-glance\">At a glance</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Modal auxiliaries</strong> (<em>can, could, may, might, must, shall, should, will, would</em>) are helping words placed before a main verb. They mark <strong>no tense</strong> and never change form (no <em>-s</em>, <em>-ing</em> or <em>to</em>). The verb after them stays bare: <em>Sachin <strong>can</strong> speak English</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Modality</strong> is the speaker’s view of a situation (Is it possible? Necessary? Allowed?), not a statement of fact. <strong>Mood</strong> codes it in grammar: the <strong>imperative</strong> (<em>Help me with these issues</em>) and the <strong>subjunctive/optative</strong> (<em>May you live long!</em>).</li>\n<li><em>Could, might, should, would</em> are <strong>independent</strong> modals. They are not the past tenses of <em>can, may, shall, will</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Can / could</strong>: <em>can</em> for present ability and general possibility; <em>could</em> for past ability, most possibilities, suggestions and the more polite request.</li>\n<li><strong>Will / would</strong>: <em>will</em> for real, future situations; <em>would</em> for imagined ones, past habits, reported speech, the second and third conditionals and formal requests.</li>\n<li><strong>Could / would</strong>: <em>could</em> for possibility, suggestions and permission; <em>would</em> for imaginary situations and offers; both for requests, but only <em>would</em> in <em>Would you mind …?</em></li>\n<li><strong>Must</strong> = necessity (a deduction) or obligation (self-imposed, non-personal, social). <strong>Shall</strong> = determination, or strong obligation in legal language. <strong>Should</strong> = advice, denial in <em>why</em>-questions, or probability.</li>\n<li><strong>May</strong> = possibility, formal permission, concession. <strong>Might</strong> = the same readings, but more <strong>remote</strong>: a weaker possibility, not a past tense.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"modal-auxiliaries-and-mood\">Modal auxiliaries and mood</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Compare two sentences from Lecture 5.1: <em>Raju likes pizza</em> and <em>Raju can eat pizza</em>. The first reports a <strong>fact</strong>. The second does not say that Raju eats pizza at all. It only says what is possible for him. The small word <em>can</em> adds the speaker’s view of the situation. Words that do this job are <strong>modal auxiliaries</strong> (or <strong>modals</strong>). The aim of the lecture: to use them “in a more conscious fashion for better confidence” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zpXHE-M5nco&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>), so that we understand others carefully and others understand us (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zpXHE-M5nco&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Auxiliary</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>An <strong>auxiliary</strong> is a helping word that comes in addition to a main verb (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-PACFxfrcgc&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>). There are two kinds (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zpXHE-M5nco&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tense auxiliaries</strong> show <strong>tense</strong>, the time of an action: <em>is, am, are</em> (present), <em>was, were</em> (past), and <em>shall, will</em> (future).</li>\n<li><strong>Modal auxiliaries</strong> (<strong>modals</strong>): <em>can, could, may, might, must, shall, should, will, would</em>. They show <strong>no tense</strong> at all.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>The tense auxiliaries, from the slide <em>Tense in English</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zpXHE-M5nco&amp;t=885s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:45</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Time</th>\n<th>Singular</th>\n<th>Plural</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Present</td>\n<td><em>is</em> (<em>am</em> with <em>I</em>)</td>\n<td><em>are</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Past</td>\n<td><em>was</em></td>\n<td><em>were</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Future</td>\n<td><em>shall / will</em></td>\n<td><em>shall / will</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><em>am</em> goes only with the first-person pronoun <em>I</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kRKZiO9QyZg&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>).</li>\n<li>As future markers, <em>shall</em> and <em>will</em> can be used interchangeably, and they have no singular–plural difference (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zpXHE-M5nco&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>).</li>\n<li>Tense and aspect in detail: <a href=\"#/english2/w02/tenses-and-aspects\">Week 2</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">“Modal verbs” or “modal auxiliaries”?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lectures 5.2–5.4 call these words <strong>modal verbs</strong>, the common name. Lectures 5.1, 5.5 and 5.6 insist that modals are <strong>not verbs</strong>: “modals are only auxiliaries; they are not verbs” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zpXHE-M5nco&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>). On the 5.1 title slide, <em>Mood and Modal Verbs</em>, the lecturer crosses out <em>Verbs</em> and writes <em>Auxiliaries</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zpXHE-M5nco&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>). Both names mean the same nine words. The reason for “auxiliary”: a modal can only come as a helper, before a main verb (<em>can <strong>speak</strong></em>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"two-sets-of-modals\">Two sets of modals</h3>\n<p>The slide <em>Modals in English</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zpXHE-M5nco&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>) arranges the nine modals in two sets. Lecture 5.2 also says the primary modals are nine in number (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qglDbdAnzkY&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>).</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Set 1</th>\n<th>Set 2</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>can</em></td>\n<td><em>could</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>may</em></td>\n<td><em>might</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>must</em></td>\n<td>(none)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>shall</em></td>\n<td><em>should</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>will</em></td>\n<td><em>would</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Set 2 is not the past tense of Set 1</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>could, might, should, would</em> look like the past forms of <em>can, may, shall, will</em>, and many people believe they are. They are not: each one is an <strong>independent</strong> modal auxiliary, and Set 2 is not derived from Set 1 (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zpXHE-M5nco&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>). Lecture 5.6 says it again: <em>might</em> is not the past tense form of <em>may</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-PACFxfrcgc&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>).</p>\n<p>The lecturer’s “simple proof” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zpXHE-M5nco&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>shall</em> and <em>will</em> are markers of the <strong>future</strong>.</li>\n<li>A future marker cannot have a past form.</li>\n<li>So <em>should</em> and <em>would</em> cannot be the past forms of <em>shall</em> and <em>will</em>. In the same way, <em>can</em> and <em>may</em> have no past forms.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Then why do <em>could</em> and <em>would</em> talk about the past?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lectures 5.2 and 5.4 use <em>could</em> and <em>would</em> for past situations: <em>I could play tennis when I was in school</em>; <em>I knew he would pass the exam</em>. One way to hold both ideas together: in these sentences the past time comes from the other words (<em>when I was in school</em>, <em>knew</em>). The modal adds only ability or prediction and carries no tense of its own. Learn the uses from Lectures 5.2–5.4, and keep the rule of Lecture 5.1: <strong>modals do not mark tense</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"modality-and-mood\">Modality and mood</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Modality</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>From the slides (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zpXHE-M5nco&amp;t=406s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:46</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zpXHE-M5nco&amp;t=640s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:40</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Modality is about <strong>non-factual</strong> things: a modal sentence does not stress being factual.</li>\n<li>It is the <strong>speaker’s comment</strong> on necessity or possibility.</li>\n<li>It deals with the <strong>meaning</strong> (the <em>semantic</em> side) of a sentence. What all modals share: they do not present a situation as a fact.</li>\n<li>Auxiliaries are <strong>one</strong> way of expressing modality.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>Two of the lecture’s examples of “non-factual” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zpXHE-M5nco&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Can I tell you something?</em> Here <em>can</em> only seeks permission. Nothing has been told yet, so nothing is reported as a fact.</li>\n<li><em>We should drive slow.</em> This states an <strong>obligation</strong> (a duty), not a fact about how we drive.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Through modals we get many readings: some give necessity, some possibility, and more (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zpXHE-M5nco&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Mood</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The word <em>modality</em> comes from <strong>mood</strong>: modality is the grammatical representation of mood (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zpXHE-M5nco&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>). The slide says: “Mood is grammatical coding of modality in verb inflections.” (An <strong>inflection</strong> is a change in a word’s form that shows grammar: <em>like → likes, liked, liking</em>.) There are two moods (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zpXHE-M5nco&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Imperative</strong>: you <strong>ask</strong> for something. <em>Help me with these issues.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Subjunctive</strong>, also called <strong>optative</strong>: you <strong>wish</strong> for something. <em>May you live long!</em></li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Modality without modals</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide <em>Adverbs and Modality</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zpXHE-M5nco&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>) shows other ways to express modality:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>non-inflecting</strong> words such as adverbs: <em>perhaps</em>, <em>in all possibility</em>;</li>\n<li><strong>hedges</strong>, phrases that soften what you say: <em>I would think (that) …</em>, <em>If I were you …</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>While this slide is on screen, the lecturer adds that modals are non-inflecting too: you cannot add <em>-ing</em> or <em>-es</em> to <em>can</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-readings-of-a-modal\">The readings of a modal</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Reading</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>reading</strong> (or <strong>interpretation</strong>) is the meaning a modal gives in a particular sentence. The slide lists six (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zpXHE-M5nco&amp;t=640s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:40</a>): <strong>possibility, ability, necessity, certainty, obligation, willingness</strong>. Inside one reading there are degrees: a possibility can be strong or weak (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zpXHE-M5nco&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s first examples (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zpXHE-M5nco&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>), with their readings:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sentence</th>\n<th>Reading</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Can</strong> I tell you something?</td>\n<td>permission (asking for it)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>What <strong>can</strong> I do for you?</td>\n<td>willingness</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I <strong>can</strong> play piano.</td>\n<td>ability</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I <strong>would</strong> love to hear from you.</td>\n<td>willingness</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>They <strong>may</strong> go to Canada next year.</td>\n<td>possibility</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>She <strong>might</strong> like these dresses.</td>\n<td>possibility</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>You <strong>can’t</strong> (cannot) be serious.</td>\n<td>certainty (the speaker is sure it is not so)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>We <strong>must</strong> study.</td>\n<td>obligation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>We <strong>should</strong> listen to others.</td>\n<td>advice</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The lecturer names the readings of sentences 1, 2, 4 and 6 (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zpXHE-M5nco&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>). The other readings in the table come from the later slides and lectures: <em>can</em> for ability, <em>may</em> for possibility, <em>must</em> for obligation, <em>should</em> for advice.</p>\n<p>One modal can have <strong>more than two readings</strong>. The slide for <em>can</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zpXHE-M5nco&amp;t=940s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:40</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sentence</th>\n<th>Reading of <em>can</em></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Anyone can become a minister.</td>\n<td>possibility</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Can I ask you a question?</td>\n<td>permission</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>She can walk fast.</td>\n<td>ability</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>They can speak several languages.</td>\n<td>ability</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>A slide shown only for a moment at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zpXHE-M5nco&amp;t=1148s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:08</a> (pause the video to read it) gives two readings each for <em>may</em> and <em>must</em>:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Modal</th>\n<th>Reading</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>may</td>\n<td>permission</td>\n<td>May I come in?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>may</td>\n<td>good probability</td>\n<td>We may visit Mexico this summer.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>must</td>\n<td>responsibility</td>\n<td>Everyone must pay taxes.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>must</td>\n<td>assumption</td>\n<td>She didn’t arrive. She must be sick.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3 id=\"how-a-modal-behaves-in-a-sentence\">How a modal behaves in a sentence</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">What makes a modal auxiliary different (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zpXHE-M5nco&amp;t=980s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:20</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>No number or person distinction</strong>: <em>I can, she can, they can</em>; never ✗ <em>she cans</em>. (<strong>Number</strong>: singular or plural. <strong>Person</strong>: <em>I/we</em> first, <em>you</em> second, <em>he/she/it/they</em> third.)</li>\n<li><strong>No tense</strong>: a modal does not mark present or past.</li>\n<li>It works <strong>separately</strong> for agreement, tense and negation.</li>\n<li><strong>No inflection</strong>: no <em>-s/-es</em>, no <em>-ing</em>, no <em>to</em>-form (the <strong>infinitive</strong>, like <em>to go</em>, <em>to come</em>). ✗ <em>to can</em>, ✗ <em>caning</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>The pattern of a sentence with a modal (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zpXHE-M5nco&amp;t=995s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:35</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Subject</th>\n<th>Modal</th>\n<th>Verb (Object)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Sachin</td>\n<td>can</td>\n<td>speak English.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>“No other auxiliary verb is needed in the context of modals.” In the lecturer’s words, “once you get one auxiliary you cannot have two”, and this is why a modal sentence shows no tense: the modal takes the auxiliary’s place, so there is no room for a tense auxiliary such as <em>does</em> or <em>did</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zpXHE-M5nco&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Negation shows where the tense is (the lecture’s slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zpXHE-M5nco&amp;t=1030s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:10</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>Raju <strong>likes</strong> pizza.</em> The present tense and the agreement mark <em>-s</em> (for the singular <em>Raju</em>) sit on the main verb.</li>\n<li>Make it negative: <em>Raju <strong>does not</strong> like pizza.</em> The negative <em>not</em> forces tense and agreement off the main verb, onto <em>does</em>. The main verb becomes the bare <em>like</em>.</li>\n<li>Past: <em>Raju <strong>liked</strong> pizza</em> → <em>Raju <strong>did not</strong> like pizza.</em> The past tense moves to <em>did</em>.</li>\n<li>Plural subject: <em>Raju and Ramu <strong>work</strong> at the Railway Station</em> → <em>Raju and Ramu <strong>do not</strong> work at the Railway Station.</em></li>\n<li>With a modal: <em>Raju <strong>can</strong> eat pizza</em> → <em>Raju <strong>cannot / can’t</strong> eat pizza</em>, and <em>Raju and Ramu can work for Railways</em> → <em>Raju and Ramu <strong>cannot</strong> work for Railways</em>. No <em>does</em> or <em>did</em> appears: there is no tense on <em>can</em> to separate.</li>\n<li>After a modal the verb stays bare. The slide marks ✗ <em>Raju can ate pizza</em> with an asterisk (*), the sign for a wrong sentence.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> with an ordinary verb, the negative brings in <em>do, does</em> or <em>did</em> to carry the tense. With a modal it does not, because the modal has no tense to give away (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zpXHE-M5nco&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Questions follow the same pattern (a slide shown for a moment at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zpXHE-M5nco&amp;t=1144s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:04</a>). Without a modal, <em>do</em> or <em>did</em> carries the tense; with a modal, the modal itself comes first:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Without a modal</th>\n<th>With a modal</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Do you like pizza?</td>\n<td>Can you eat pizza?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Did they like pizza?</td>\n<td>Can they eat pizza?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>What do you like?</td>\n<td>What can they say?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Modals in a tensed sentence (slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zpXHE-M5nco&amp;t=1149s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:09</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Modals can be used in a tensed sentence but cannot be used as tense markers.” The slide’s examples talk about the past with <strong>modal + <em>have</em> + past participle</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It must have been a difficult decision.</li>\n<li>They should have invited her to their wedding.</li>\n<li>He must have been in a traffic jam.</li>\n<li>Joe may / might have taken the wrong train.</li>\n<li>You could have played better.</li>\n<li>You should / ought to have warned me earlier.</li>\n<li>He shouldn’t have told them.</li>\n<li>I wouldn’t have survived.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The past time comes from <em>have</em> + the past participle (the third form of a verb, V3: <em>been, invited, taken</em>), not from the modal. V3 forms: <a href=\"#/english2/w02/regular-and-irregular-verb-forms\">Week 2</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">The lecturer’s exercise (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zpXHE-M5nco&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Make a list of what this lecture discussed.</li>\n<li>Write down a few sentences that you read or that come to your mind.</li>\n<li>Underline the modals.</li>\n<li>Write the reading of each: what is it trying to convey?</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Spend 15–20 minutes on it. The <em>Practice</em> slide adds situations to try: possibilities, necessities, advice/suggestions, ability, permissions (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zpXHE-M5nco&amp;t=1152s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:12</a>). Such exercises stay in your subconscious and show up when you speak and write (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zpXHE-M5nco&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Is <em>should</em> the past tense of <em>shall</em>?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>No.</strong> <em>should</em> is an independent modal auxiliary. The lecturer’s proof: <em>shall</em> marks the future, and a future marker cannot have a past form. Modals carry no tense at all (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zpXHE-M5nco&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which mood is <em>Help me with these issues</em>, and which is <em>May you live long!</em>?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><em>Help me with these issues</em> asks for something: <strong>imperative</strong>. <em>May you live long!</em> wishes for something: <strong>subjunctive</strong> (optative).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Why is <em>Raju can ate pizza</em> wrong? Give the right sentence.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>A modal carries no tense, and the verb after it is always the bare form. So the past form <em>ate</em> cannot follow <em>can</em>. ✓ <em>Raju can eat pizza.</em></p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">What is the reading of <em>can</em> in <em>Anyone can become a minister</em>? And in <em>She can walk fast</em>?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><em>Anyone can become a minister</em>: <strong>possibility</strong>. <em>She can walk fast</em>: <strong>ability</strong>. One modal, more than one reading (the 5.1 slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zpXHE-M5nco&amp;t=940s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:40</a>).</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"can-and-could\">Can and could</h2>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Modal verb (Lecture 5.2’s slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qglDbdAnzkY&amp;t=40s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:40</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“A modal verb is a type of verb that is used to indicate modality – that is likelihood, ability, request, capacity, suggestion, order, obligation or advice.” Examples: <em>can, may, must, shall, will, could, might</em>; the primary modal verbs are nine in number.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Can and could have the same meaning”, says the slide. People mix them up in sentences, which is why this lecture exists (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qglDbdAnzkY&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>). The two differ in three ways (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qglDbdAnzkY&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>present and past</strong>: <em>can</em> for the present, <em>could</em> for the past;</li>\n<li><strong>informal and formal</strong>: <em>could</em> is the more formal, more polite word;</li>\n<li><strong>strong and weak</strong>: some modals say a thing strongly, others in a milder way.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"ability\">Ability</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Time</th>\n<th>Modal</th>\n<th>Lecture examples (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qglDbdAnzkY&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Present</td>\n<td><em>can</em></td>\n<td>I can play tennis. He can swim.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Past</td>\n<td><em>could</em></td>\n<td>I could play tennis when I was in school. He could beat anyone in swimming in his prime.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><em>I could play tennis when I was in school</em> is an ability in the past. This person does not play tennis any more (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qglDbdAnzkY&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>).</li>\n<li><em>In his prime</em> means in his best years, when he was young, healthy and energetic. Now he is not as good as he was (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qglDbdAnzkY&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"possibility\">Possibility</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\"><em>Could</em> for possibility, <em>can</em> for general possibility (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qglDbdAnzkY&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>For possibility both can be used, but <strong>could</strong> is used far more often. <strong>Can</strong> is used only for <strong>general possibilities</strong>: things that are possible in the world in general.</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Time</th>\n<th>Lecture example</th>\n<th>What it means</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Present</td>\n<td>The bus could be late.</td>\n<td>We are waiting at the bus stop and the bus is not here: a guess about now.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Future</td>\n<td>If you don’t study, then you could fail the exam.</td>\n<td>Failing is possible, not sure. You might also pass.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Past</td>\n<td>He could have been successful in his career, had he listened to his boss.</td>\n<td><em>had he listened</em> = <em>if he had listened</em>. He did not listen, so he was not successful: a past possibility that did not happen.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>General possibilities, with <em>can</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qglDbdAnzkY&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>You can fall sick if you get drenched in rain.</li>\n<li>Living in New York can cost a fortune.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"polite-expressions\">Polite expressions</h3>\n<p>The lecture puts polite expressions in four groups. Suggestions and requests are explained aloud (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qglDbdAnzkY&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qglDbdAnzkY&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>); offers and permission are on a slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qglDbdAnzkY&amp;t=666s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:06</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Use</th>\n<th>Modal</th>\n<th>Lecture examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Suggestion</td>\n<td><em>could</em></td>\n<td>We could try Thai food this time. We could ask the teacher to postpone the exam.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Request</td>\n<td><em>can</em> and <em>could</em></td>\n<td>Can / Could you pass me the salt, please? Can / Could I speak to Vaiga, please?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Offer</td>\n<td><em>can</em> and <em>could</em></td>\n<td>Can / could I get you something to drink? Can / could I help you with that, madam?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Permission</td>\n<td><em>can</em> and <em>could</em></td>\n<td>Can / Could I borrow your notes, please? Can / Could I take a day off this week?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The slide’s note: <strong>could is more polite than can</strong>.</p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Let <em>please</em> and the setting decide</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture’s question (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qglDbdAnzkY&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>): which word fills <em>____ I speak to Vaiga?</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li>There is no <em>please</em>, so the context looks <strong>informal</strong>: <em>Can I speak to Vaiga?</em> fits best. <em>Could</em> is not wrong.</li>\n<li>Now add <em>please</em>: <em>____ I speak to Vaiga, please?</em> The <em>please</em> marks a <strong>formal</strong> context, so <em>could</em> is right, because <em>could</em> is more polite than <em>can</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qglDbdAnzkY&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>).</li>\n<li><em>Can</em> is fine with a friendly boss you share a good rapport with. In unfamiliar or formal situations, and in countries where people speak English natively (mostly Britain and the US, where people are particular about polite speech), prefer <em>could</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qglDbdAnzkY&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-lecture-s-activity\">The lecture’s activity</h3>\n<p>Fill each blank with <em>can</em> or <em>could</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qglDbdAnzkY&amp;t=712s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:52</a>). The answers are at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qglDbdAnzkY&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>.</p>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Daughter: “Mom, the phone is ringing.” Mom: “____ you answer it? It ____ be grandpa.”</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Can</strong> you answer it? A request between mother and daughter is informal, so <em>can</em> is fine (<em>could</em> does no harm).</li>\n<li>It <strong>could</strong> be grandpa. This is a possibility in this particular situation, not a general possibility in the world, so only <em>could</em> works.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">I ____ run three kilometres without a break when I was a kid.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>could</strong>. <em>when I was a kid</em> puts the ability in the past, and <em>can</em> cannot be used for the past.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Many swimmers ____ hold their breath for more than 3 minutes under water.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>can</strong>. This is a general possibility in the world, not tied to one situation.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">____ I get you something to eat?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Can</strong> (or <em>could</em>). The setting sounds informal and there is no <em>please</em>, so the answer slide gives <em>Can</em>; <em>could</em> is also fine. The lecturer calls it a request; it has the same pattern as the slide’s offer <em>Can/could I get you something to drink?</em></p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">____ you send me the details by email, please?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Could</strong>. <em>please</em> marks a formal request, and <em>could</em> is more polite than <em>can</em>.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"will-and-would\">Will and would</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>English learners mix up <em>will</em> and <em>would</em> because the two appear in similar situations, but they are not the same (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bLYW1Xqe2Cg&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>). The main difference:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>will</strong> is used for <strong>real</strong> situations, and it always points to the <strong>future</strong>;</li>\n<li><strong>would</strong> is used for <strong>imagined</strong> situations, in the future or in the past.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The overview slide lists the areas where the choice matters: conditionals, making requests, offering something, giving advice, and reported speech. The lecture develops all of them except advice.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"five-uses-of-will\">Five uses of <em>will</em></h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Use (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bLYW1Xqe2Cg&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>)</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Give information about the future</td>\n<td>We’ll head home after work.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Say what we think or guess will happen</td>\n<td>Don’t lend him your car. He’ll crash it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Say what we are ready to do, without any objection</td>\n<td>I will eat anything. I’m not picky at all.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>State general rules: signs, codes of conduct, company policies and procedures</td>\n<td>Smokers will be asked to smoke outside.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Give orders or commands</td>\n<td>You will tidy up your room.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em>'ll</em> is the short form of <em>will</em> (<em>we’ll</em> = <em>we will</em>). <em>Picky</em> means hard to please about food.</p>\n<h3 id=\"would-for-the-past\"><em>Would</em> for the past</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Use (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bLYW1Xqe2Cg&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>)</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>What we expected to happen in the past</td>\n<td>The sky was getting cloudy, which meant it would rain.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Habits in the past</td>\n<td>When we lived in the mountains, we would go hiking all the time.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\"><em>Would</em> for a past habit needs a past setting</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>We used to smoke</em> or <em>We would smoke</em>? The lecture prefers <em>We used to smoke</em>. Without a clear past setting, <em>We would smoke</em> can sound like something we might do <strong>now</strong> if we had the opportunity. For clarity, give the past time: <em>Before we quit, we would smoke all the time</em>. Or use <em>used to</em>: <em>We used to smoke all the time</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bLYW1Xqe2Cg&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"conditionals\">Conditionals</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Conditional</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>conditional</strong> is an <em>if</em>-sentence (“sometimes we call them the ‘if clause’”). The <em>if</em>-part gives a <strong>condition</strong>; the other part gives the <strong>result</strong>: of something that might happen now or in the future, or that could have happened in the past but did not (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bLYW1Xqe2Cg&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>). Each type uses its own pattern of tenses.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Short forms on the slide: <em>PrS</em> = present simple (<em>rains, add</em>); <em>PstS</em> = past simple (<em>had</em>); <em>PstPerfect</em> = past perfect (<em>had studied</em>). “Infinitive” here means the base verb without <em>to</em> (<em>stay, change</em>).</p>\n<p>The four types in the lecture: zero (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bLYW1Xqe2Cg&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>), first (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bLYW1Xqe2Cg&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>), second (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bLYW1Xqe2Cg&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>) and third (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bLYW1Xqe2Cg&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>).</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type: talks about</th>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n<th>Lecture example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Zero</strong>: facts that are always true</td>\n<td>if + present simple, present simple</td>\n<td>If you add one and one, you get two.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>First</strong>: future events that are likely</td>\n<td>if + present simple, <strong>will</strong> + base verb</td>\n<td>If it rains tomorrow, I’ll stay at home.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Second</strong>: impossible things now, or unlikely things in the future</td>\n<td>if + past simple, <strong>would</strong> + base verb</td>\n<td>If I had a magic wand, I would change some historical events.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Third</strong>: past things that did not happen, and their imaginary results</td>\n<td>if + past perfect, <strong>would have</strong> + past participle</td>\n<td>If I had studied harder, I would have passed the exam.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">No modal, <em>will</em>, <em>would</em>, <em>would have</em></span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>Zero conditional: <strong>no</strong> modal at all. The example has neither <em>will</em> nor <em>would</em>, “precisely the point” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bLYW1Xqe2Cg&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>).</li>\n<li>First: <em>will</em>.</li>\n<li>Second: <em>would</em>, not <em>will</em>. ✗ <em>If I had a magic wand, I will change some historical events.</em></li>\n<li>Third: <em>would have</em> + past participle. The slide writes “would + past participle”; the example shows the full form, <em>would have passed</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"requests-and-proposals\">Requests and proposals</h3>\n<p>A <strong>proposal</strong> here means offering or suggesting something (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bLYW1Xqe2Cg&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>).</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Setting</th>\n<th>Modal</th>\n<th>Examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Formal</td>\n<td><em>would</em></td>\n<td>Would you please come in? Would you like a cup of coffee?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Informal</td>\n<td><em>will</em></td>\n<td>Will you come in? Will you have a cup of coffee?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>(The slide prints <em>Will you like a cup of coffee?</em>; the lecturer says <em>Will you have a cup of coffee?</em>)</p>\n<h3 id=\"reported-speech-and-past-habits\">Reported speech and past habits</h3>\n<p><strong>Reported speech</strong> tells what someone said, in our own words. In reported speech and in descriptions of past habits, use <strong>would</strong>, never <em>will</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bLYW1Xqe2Cg&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reported speech: <em>She told me that she would be here at 8 o’clock.</em></li>\n<li>Past habit: <em>When I was young, I would play tennis with my brother.</em> ✗ <em>When I was young, I will play tennis with my brother</em> sounds absurd.</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\"><em>Would</em> goes with action verbs, not state verbs</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>state verb</strong> describes a state of being, not an action; the lecture’s example is <em>live</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bLYW1Xqe2Cg&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>). <em>Would</em> for past habits works only with <strong>action verbs</strong>, such as <em>play</em>. With a state verb, use <em>used to</em>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>✗ When I was young, I would live in an old house.</li>\n<li>✓ When I was young, I used to live in an old house.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"more-uses-of-would\">More uses of <em>would</em></h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Use</th>\n<th>Lecture examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Liking and preferences, with <em>love, hate, prefer, like, glad</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bLYW1Xqe2Cg&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>)</td>\n<td>I would be glad to help. I would hate to work out in the morning. I would love to go on a trip to Spain.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Opinion on uncertain things (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bLYW1Xqe2Cg&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>)</td>\n<td>I would say he is about forty. I think it would take about forty minutes.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Refusal to do something (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bLYW1Xqe2Cg&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>)</td>\n<td>Reva wouldn’t go to the doctor, even though she was in pain. My bike would not start today.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>After <em>wish</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bLYW1Xqe2Cg&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>)</td>\n<td>I wish she would leave me. I wish you would be quiet for a minute.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Result or intention, with <em>so that</em> / <em>in order</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bLYW1Xqe2Cg&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>)</td>\n<td>He burned all the letters so that his mother would never read them.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Opinion</strong>: a friend asks, “How old do you think this man is?” Say <em>I would say he is about 40</em>, not <em>I will say he is about 40</em>. Asked how long it takes to travel from Velachery to Adyar, say <em>I think it would take about 20 minutes</em>. <em>I think</em> shows that you are giving an opinion on something you are not sure about, so use <em>would</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bLYW1Xqe2Cg&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Refusal</strong>: Reva was in pain in the past, so <em>wouldn’t</em> (= refused to) fits, not <em>Reva will not go …</em>. <em>My bike will not start today</em> sounds as if you had a vision in bed that your bike will not start. Unless the situation is that unusual, say <em>My bike would not start today</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bLYW1Xqe2Cg&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>wish</strong>: ✗ <em>I wish she will leave me.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Result</strong>: ✗ <em>He burnt all the letters so that his mother will never read them</em> “does not sound very right”.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These situations cover almost all the uses of <em>will</em> and <em>would</em>. Making your own examples like them is a great way to improve your writing and speaking (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bLYW1Xqe2Cg&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>).</p>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Zero, first, second or third conditional? <em>If it rains tomorrow, I’ll stay at home.</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>First.</strong> The pattern is if + present simple (<em>rains</em>), <em>will</em> + base verb (<em>stay</em>): a future event that is likely.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">What is wrong with <em>If I had a magic wand, I will change some historical events</em>?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><em>If I had</em> (if + past simple) makes it a <strong>second</strong> conditional: an impossible, imagined situation. The result needs <em>would</em>: ✓ <em>I would change some historical events.</em></p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\"><em>I will say he is about forty</em> or <em>I would say he is about forty</em>?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><em><strong>I would say …</strong></em> You are giving an opinion on something uncertain, and <em>would</em> is the modal for that.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"could-and-would\">Could and would</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Could</em> and <em>would</em> look alike and both appear in polite speech, so they are easy to mix up. Lecture 5.4 starts from what they share (both can talk about the past) and then separates them in two areas: <strong>possible versus imaginary</strong> situations, and four kinds of <strong>polite expressions</strong> (suggestions, offers, requests, permission). It ends with an activity.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"both-can-talk-about-the-past\">Both can talk about the past</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Present or future</th>\n<th>Past</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>I can run a few kilometres without any break. (present ability)</td>\n<td>I could run a few kilometres without any break.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I know he will pass the exam. (future)</td>\n<td>I knew he would pass the exam.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><em>I could run …</em>: he does not have this ability now (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=meJgUe5W6Yg&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>).</li>\n<li><em>I knew he would pass …</em>: his belief in the past. He was sure, even then, that he would pass (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=meJgUe5W6Yg&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"possible-or-imaginary\">Possible or imaginary?</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Possibility → <em>could</em>; imaginary situation → <em>would</em></span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>For a <strong>possible</strong> situation, use <em>could</em>, never <em>would</em>. Here <em>could</em> “indicates possibility and not past form – would cannot be used in place of could” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=meJgUe5W6Yg&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>).</li>\n<li>For an <strong>imaginary</strong> situation, prefer <em>would</em>. <em>Could</em> is also acceptable, but <em>would</em> is used a lot more (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=meJgUe5W6Yg&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Possible: <em>could</em> only</th>\n<th>Imaginary: <em>would</em> preferred</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Miss Maya has set the question paper. The exam could be very tough.</td>\n<td>If I had a lot of money, I would retire early. (Also: I could retire early.)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>My boss isn’t responding to my mails. He could be busy.</td>\n<td>If Kishore worked hard, he would pass the exam. (Also: he could pass the exam.)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>✗ <em>The exam would be very tough.</em> ✗ <em>He would be busy.</em> “A lot of us confuse it” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=meJgUe5W6Yg&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"polite-expressions-2\">Polite expressions</h3>\n<p>The lecture takes four kinds in turn: suggestions (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=meJgUe5W6Yg&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>), offers (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=meJgUe5W6Yg&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>), requests (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=meJgUe5W6Yg&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>), with the fixed phrase <em>would you mind</em> at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=meJgUe5W6Yg&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>, and permission (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=meJgUe5W6Yg&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>).</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Use</th>\n<th>Right</th>\n<th>Wrong</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Suggestion: <em>could</em></td>\n<td>We could try Thai food for dinner.</td>\n<td>✗ We would try Thai food for dinner.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Offer: <em>would</em></td>\n<td>Would you like some coffee?</td>\n<td>✗ Could you like some tea?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Request: both</td>\n<td>Could / Would you please pass it on? Could / Would you please open this door for me?</td>\n<td>(none)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fixed phrase: <em>would</em></td>\n<td>Would you mind opening the window, please?</td>\n<td>✗ Could you mind opening the window, please?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Permission: <em>could</em></td>\n<td>Could I borrow your pen for a couple of minutes?</td>\n<td>✗ Would I borrow your pen?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>With <em>would</em>, a request becomes <strong>very formal</strong>, and polite: using <em>would</em> is “a mark of politeness” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=meJgUe5W6Yg&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>).</li>\n<li><em>Would you mind</em> is a <strong>fixed phrase</strong> (a group of words always used in the same form). Its <em>would</em> can never be replaced by <em>could</em>.</li>\n<li>To ask permission with <em>would</em>, start with <em>Would it be OK if …</em> or <em>Would you mind if …</em>, and put the next verb in the <strong>past</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=meJgUe5W6Yg&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>):\n<ul>\n<li>Would it be OK if I <strong>borrowed</strong> your bike for a couple of days?</li>\n<li>Would you mind if I <strong>borrowed</strong> your bike for a couple of days?</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Two kinds of offer</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 5.2 lists offers with <em>can/could</em> (<em>Can/could I get you something to drink?</em>), and Lecture 5.4 says offers take <em>would</em> (<em>Would you like some coffee?</em>). They are two different patterns:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Can / Could <strong>I</strong> + verb …?</em> offers to <strong>do</strong> something for the other person.</li>\n<li><em>Would <strong>you</strong> like …?</em> asks what the other person <strong>wants</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Could you like …?</em> is always wrong.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-lecture-s-activity-2\">The lecture’s activity</h3>\n<p>Fill each blank with <em>could</em> or <em>would</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=meJgUe5W6Yg&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>). The answers are at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=meJgUe5W6Yg&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>.</p>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">____ you mind sharing your notes with me?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Would.</strong> <em>Would you mind</em> is a fixed phrase, and <em>could</em> cannot replace its <em>would</em>.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Prabha ____ already speak three languages when she was 6.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>could</strong>: an ability in the past (<em>when she was 6</em>).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">If I became the Prime Minister, I ____ make health services free for everybody.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>would</strong>: an imaginary situation (if + past simple, <em>would</em> + base verb), where <em>would</em> is preferred.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">____ I use your laptop to send an email?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Could</strong>: you are asking for permission. ✗ <em>Would I use your laptop …?</em></p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">____ you like me to give you a ride back home?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Would</strong>: you are making an offer. (The question slide prints <em>want</em>; the answer slide reads <em>Would you like me to give you a ride back home?</em>)</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"must-shall-and-should\">Must, shall and should</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Must</em>, <em>shall</em> and <em>should</em> are probably the most frequently used modals. Knowing their specific uses improves how we talk, how we sound, and how others see our communication skills (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kRKZiO9QyZg&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>). The lecture repeats the key point of Lecture 5.1: modals are not verbs but auxiliaries, and unlike the tense auxiliaries (<em>is, are, am, was, were</em>) they never carry a tense marker (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kRKZiO9QyZg&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"must-for-necessity\"><em>Must</em> for necessity</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Must: necessity (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kRKZiO9QyZg&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“The core meaning of ‘must’ is that of necessity.” The speaker uses what he or she <strong>knows and is aware of</strong> to make a <strong>deduction</strong> (a conclusion drawn from evidence), and this conclusion is seen as a necessity. Nothing in the grammar says “necessity”; it comes from our knowledge of the situation (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kRKZiO9QyZg&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s examples (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kRKZiO9QyZg&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>What the speaker knows or sees</th>\n<th>The deduction</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Look at how thick that canopy is.</td>\n<td>The trees must have many branches.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>There is no room-heater.</td>\n<td>It must be too cold in there without the room-heater.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The fan is not working.</td>\n<td>It must be power failure.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>A <strong>canopy</strong> is the roof of leaves and branches above a forest.</p>\n<h3 id=\"must-for-obligation\"><em>Must</em> for obligation</h3>\n<p>The second reading of <em>must</em> is <strong>obligation</strong> (a duty). It has three types (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kRKZiO9QyZg&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>): self-imposed (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kRKZiO9QyZg&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>), non-personal (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kRKZiO9QyZg&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>) and social (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kRKZiO9QyZg&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>).</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n<th>Lecture example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Self-imposed</td>\n<td>a <strong>weak</strong> obligation; a first-person subject (<em>I, we</em>); mostly a reminder to oneself</td>\n<td>I must visit him today. I must clean the room.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Non-personal</td>\n<td>warnings and rules; it involves human performers of the action; said in an impersonal way, not to one person directly</td>\n<td>Submissions must follow the guidelines as instructed by the scientific committee.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Social</td>\n<td>it has tones of necessity, but it is vague whether the event will actually happen</td>\n<td>We are going to Bangalore tomorrow. We must visit Ramesh’s uncle and his family.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Self-imposed</strong>: when I say <em>I must clean my room</em>, even to you, I am mostly telling <strong>myself</strong>: “talking to ourselves” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kRKZiO9QyZg&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Social</strong>: the visit depends on the earlier information, <em>we are going to Bangalore tomorrow</em>. “Vague” means it is not sure that the visit will happen (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kRKZiO9QyZg&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Every modal statement is hypothetical</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“All information through a modal auxiliary is of that type in nature”: hypothetical. <em>Submissions must follow the guidelines</em> states a rule, but that the submissions will actually follow it is not guaranteed (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kRKZiO9QyZg&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"mustn-t\"><em>Mustn’t</em></h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Mustn’t (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kRKZiO9QyZg&amp;t=710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:50</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Mustn’t</em> (<em>must not</em>) is the negative form of <em>must</em>. It combines necessity <strong>and</strong> obligation, so the obligation becomes <strong>forceful</strong>: there is no option.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>You mustn’t be distracted while driving.</li>\n<li>You mustn’t use your phones while at a fuel station.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"shall\"><em>Shall</em></h3>\n<p><em>Shall</em> has two uses (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kRKZiO9QyZg&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a <strong>tense auxiliary</strong> for the future, with limited uses. Only in this sense are <em>shall</em> and <em>will</em> interchangeable;</li>\n<li>a <strong>modal auxiliary</strong>. Then it is not a substitute for <em>will</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Shall as a modal (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kRKZiO9QyZg&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>In contemporary (present-day) English, <em>shall</em> is not just a substitute for <em>will</em> with the first person (<em>I, we</em>).</li>\n<li>In first-person use, <em>shall</em> shows <strong>determination</strong> on the part of the speaker. <em>Will</em> does not have the same force of determination.</li>\n<li><em>Shall</em> can also mean <strong>intention</strong>, <strong>willingness</strong> and <strong>prediction</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Example (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kRKZiO9QyZg&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>)</th>\n<th>Reading</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>I shall talk to you about the proposal tomorrow.</td>\n<td>intention</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shall we go?</td>\n<td>willingness</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>We shall have finished the work by noon.</td>\n<td>prediction</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3 id=\"shall-for-strong-obligation\"><em>Shall</em> for strong obligation</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Shall: strong obligation (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kRKZiO9QyZg&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Shall</em> has a socially oriented use with the reading of a <strong>strong obligation</strong>, especially in <strong>legal</strong> language. It can be used between people too.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The doctors who do not abide by the guidelines of IMA for practising medicine shall have their license revoked with immediate effect.</li>\n<li>The petitioner shall present an affidavit in regards to the issue submitted to this court.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>Word help: <em>IMA</em> = Indian Medical Association; <em>abide by</em> = follow; <em>revoked</em> = cancelled; <em>petitioner</em> = a person who asks a court for something; <em>affidavit</em> = a written statement sworn to be true. “Sounds like law”, says the lecturer. Using such distinctions in writing and speech makes us impactful (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kRKZiO9QyZg&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"should\"><em>Should</em></h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Should: requirement (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kRKZiO9QyZg&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“The core interpretation of ‘should’ is that of requirement.” It is mostly used in <strong>socially rooted</strong> contexts, to show a <strong>weaker</strong> obligation. Then it states what behaviour is appropriate, as <strong>advice or suggestion</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>You should prepare well for the learner’s test.</li>\n<li>You should inform me before you leave.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<ul>\n<li><em>You should prepare well for the learner’s test</em>: not a strong obligation, but if you want to pass, you should prepare. (The <em>learner’s test</em> is the test for a learner’s driving licence.)</li>\n<li><em>You should inform me before you leave</em>: a requirement. Leaving without telling is not good behaviour (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kRKZiO9QyZg&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Why + should = denial (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kRKZiO9QyZg&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Should</em> in a <em>why</em>-question questions or <strong>denies</strong> the relevance of a social requirement. It does not really ask for reasons. It is a strong denial, with almost no chance that the speaker will do it.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Why should I see the movie if I don’t like it?</li>\n<li>Why should I do what everyone else does?</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Should: probability (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kRKZiO9QyZg&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Should</em> can also state a requirement in terms of <strong>what most probably is the case</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>This work should take two or three days.</li>\n<li>Your order should be delivered in six to seven days.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p><em>This work must take two or three days</em>: “grammatically that might be accurate but contextually that is not appropriate”. <em>Should</em> leaves the statement to probability (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kRKZiO9QyZg&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>). The lecturer calls this, too, a weak obligation.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Modal: core reading</th>\n<th>How strong?</th>\n<th>Lecture example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em><strong>must</strong></em>: necessity; obligation</td>\n<td>strong (forceful as <em>mustn’t</em>); weak when self-imposed</td>\n<td>You mustn’t use your phones while at a fuel station.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em><strong>shall</strong></em>: determination; obligation in law</td>\n<td>strong</td>\n<td>The petitioner shall present an affidavit …</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em><strong>should</strong></em>: requirement</td>\n<td>weak: advice</td>\n<td>You should inform me before you leave.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which type of obligation is <em>Submissions must follow the guidelines as instructed by the scientific committee</em>?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Non-personal.</strong> It is a rule, there is no first-person subject, and it is said impersonally, not to one person directly.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Is <em>I must clean the room</em> a strong or a weak obligation? Why?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Weak</strong>: it is <strong>self-imposed</strong>. The subject is the first person <em>I</em>, and the speaker is mostly reminding himself or herself.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\"><em>It must be power failure</em>: necessity or obligation?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Necessity.</strong> The speaker sees that the fan is not working and deduces the cause. Nobody is given a duty.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Why does the lecture prefer <em>This work should take two or three days</em> to <em>This work must take two or three days</em>?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>Both are grammatical, but <em>must</em> is not appropriate in this context. <em>Should</em> states what most probably is the case and leaves room for probability.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"may-and-might\">May and might</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Grammar gives <strong>accuracy</strong>: correct sentences. Beyond accuracy is <strong>appropriateness</strong>: a correct sentence that also suits the situation and the people. This lecture helps you be precise with <em>may</em> and <em>might</em> when you speak and write, and helps you understand people who grew up speaking English (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-PACFxfrcgc&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-PACFxfrcgc&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>). Its advice: pause, look at the details of the examples, and create your own (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-PACFxfrcgc&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>A recap first. An auxiliary is an element that comes in addition to other verbs; there are tense auxiliaries and modal auxiliaries (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-PACFxfrcgc&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>). <em>Might</em> is not derived from <em>may</em>, and it is not the past tense of <em>may</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-PACFxfrcgc&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>). A footnote: modals may appear in sentences that have tense, but they do not denote tense themselves (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-PACFxfrcgc&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"may-for-possibility\"><em>May</em> for possibility</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">May: possibility (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-PACFxfrcgc&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“The core meaning of ‘may’ is that of possibility.” Functionally, <em>may</em> says that an event has an <strong>equal possibility</strong> of happening or not. Sometimes the sentence states both sides.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The delivery may be delayed.</li>\n<li>They may call us tomorrow or they may not.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p><em>The delivery will be delayed</em> is <strong>certain</strong>. When that certainty is missing, we say <em>may be delayed</em>: it may be delayed, or it may not (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-PACFxfrcgc&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Literal meaning and pragmatic meaning</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Pragmatics</strong> is about meaning in a real situation, beyond the words themselves. Grammatically (literally), <em>The delivery may be delayed</em> means only “possibility”. Pragmatically, we use <em>may</em> most often when we already <strong>know</strong> the delivery will be delayed: <em>may</em> is a polite way of giving the news (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-PACFxfrcgc&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"may-for-permission\"><em>May</em> for permission</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">May: permission (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-PACFxfrcgc&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The second (secondary) reading of <em>may</em> is <strong>permission</strong>. It belongs to situations of social <strong>authority and power</strong>: <em>may</em> highlights a difference in social power or hierarchy between the people who ask for or give permission. It makes the sentence formal and polite.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>May I take a tea break?</li>\n<li>You may carry your handbags inside the room.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>In the second sentence, someone is politely allowing you to take your bags inside (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-PACFxfrcgc&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">May or can for permission (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-PACFxfrcgc&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In everyday spoken English, <em>may</em> for permission is more common in <strong>formal</strong> contexts. <em>Can</em> replaces it in casual, <strong>informal</strong> conversation.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>May I go to the theatre? (permission, formal)</li>\n<li>Can I go to the theatre? (permission, informal)</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>Why does <em>can</em> sound informal? <em>Can</em> carries a sense of <strong>ability</strong>: <em>I can run</em> shows my capability to run (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-PACFxfrcgc&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>). In a formal request we take away our own ability and authority. Asking with <em>may</em> shows “a sense of surrender and total dependence” on the other person, and that makes it formal (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-PACFxfrcgc&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"may-for-concession\"><em>May</em> for concession</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Concession (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-PACFxfrcgc&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Concession</strong> means <strong>acknowledging</strong> something: the speaker accepts that an event is possible (or not), yet does not necessarily agree that it matters for the current discussion.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The school may be open on Sundays, but the children will not go.</li>\n<li>He may be a child, but he is very wise.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>The school may be open for some other, technical reason. That does not change the main point: the children will not go (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-PACFxfrcgc&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>). Concession does not remove the reading of possibility.</p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">The pattern <em>may …, but …</em></span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>may</em> + the point you accept, then <em>but</em> + the point that matters.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"might\"><em>Might</em></h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Might (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-PACFxfrcgc&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><em>Might</em>, like <em>may</em>, shows <strong>possibility</strong> and <strong>permission</strong>. That is why it seems related to <em>may</em>.</li>\n<li>The difference is <strong>relative remoteness</strong>: <em>may</em> talks about a <strong>recent</strong> (closer) possibility, <em>might</em> about a <strong>remote</strong> one. It is not present versus past (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-PACFxfrcgc&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>).</li>\n<li>So <em>might</em> shows a <strong>weaker possibility</strong> than <em>may</em>. This gives a sense of <strong>uncertainty</strong> about whether an event will happen or a permission is granted.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<ul>\n<li><em>The Vice-Chancellor may come tomorrow</em>: he may or may not. <em>The Vice-Chancellor might come tomorrow</em>: a very weak possibility, leaning towards “he will not” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-PACFxfrcgc&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>).</li>\n<li><em>She is at home now, but she might reach office very soon.</em></li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Might: hypothetical</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The remoteness of <em>might</em> can also give a <strong>hypothetical</strong> (imagined) or <strong>unreal</strong> reading (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-PACFxfrcgc&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>She is a bright student, so she might become successful in life.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>On the slide the lecturer marks <em>might</em> as the auxiliary and <em>become</em> as the verb: <em>might</em> adds a remote possibility to <em>become</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-PACFxfrcgc&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Might: concession (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-PACFxfrcgc&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In concession, <em>might</em> refers to an <strong>even less likely</strong> or <strong>less relevant</strong> situation.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>This project might happen, but we are not going to talk about it now.</li>\n<li>That event might not take place, but we are not worried about it.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Reading</th>\n<th><em>may</em></th>\n<th><em>might</em></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Possibility</td>\n<td>equal chance: <em>The delivery may be delayed.</em></td>\n<td>weaker, remote: <em>She might reach office very soon.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Permission</td>\n<td>formal: <em>May I take a tea break?</em></td>\n<td>also permission, says the slide (no example given)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Concession</td>\n<td><em>He may be a child, but he is very wise.</em></td>\n<td>less likely or less relevant: <em>This project might happen, but we are not going to talk about it now.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hypothetical</td>\n<td>(not discussed)</td>\n<td><em>She is a bright student, so she might become successful in life.</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>To conclude (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-PACFxfrcgc&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>): <em>may</em> and <em>might</em> have similar readings (possibility, permission and concession), but one is <strong>recent</strong> and the other <strong>remote</strong>. <em>Might</em> is a polite way of referring to impossibility, although literally it still contains possibility.</p>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\"><em>May I take a tea break?</em> or <em>Can I take a tea break?</em>: which is formal, and why?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><em><strong>May I …?</strong></em> is formal. <em>Can</em> carries a sense of ability, so it sounds casual. <em>May</em> shows the speaker depending on someone with authority, which is formal and polite.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which reading does <em>may</em> have in <em>He may be a child, but he is very wise</em>?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Concession.</strong> The speaker accepts that he is a child, but says that this does not matter for the point being made: he is very wise.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">How does <em>The Vice-Chancellor might come tomorrow</em> differ from <em>The Vice-Chancellor may come tomorrow</em>?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><em>might</em> is more <strong>remote</strong>: a weaker possibility, leaning towards “he will not come”. <em>may</em> leaves an equal chance either way. Both talk about tomorrow, so <em>might</em> is not a past tense here.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"summary\">Summary</h2>\n<section class=\"box sm\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">In short</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Auxiliaries</strong>: tense auxiliaries (<em>is, am, are, was, were</em>; <em>shall/will</em> for the future) carry tense; modal auxiliaries (<em>can, could, may, might, must, shall, should, will, would</em>) do not.</li>\n<li><strong>Modals</strong>: no tense, no number or person forms, no <em>-s</em>, <em>-ing</em> or <em>to</em>; the bare verb follows; no <em>do/does/did</em> in negatives or questions (<em>Raju cannot eat pizza</em>). <em>Could, might, should, would</em> are independent modals, not past tenses. Past time: modal + <em>have</em> + V3 (<em>It must have been a difficult decision</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Modality</strong>: the speaker’s non-factual comment. Readings: possibility, ability, necessity, certainty, obligation, willingness. <strong>Mood</strong>: imperative (<em>Help me with these issues</em>), subjunctive/optative (<em>May you live long!</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Conditionals</strong>: zero (no modal), first (<em>will</em>), second (<em>would</em>), third (<em>would have</em> + V3).</li>\n<li><strong>Which modal when:</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>To express</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n<th>Lecture example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>ability, now / in the past</td>\n<td><em>can</em> / <em>could</em></td>\n<td>I could play tennis when I was in school.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>possibility in a situation</td>\n<td><em>could</em>, <em>may</em>, <em>might</em> (weakest)</td>\n<td>The bus could be late.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>general possibility</td>\n<td><em>can</em></td>\n<td>Living in New York can cost a fortune.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>suggestion</td>\n<td><em>could</em></td>\n<td>We could try Thai food for dinner.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>offer</td>\n<td><em>Would you like …?</em>; <em>Can/Could I …?</em></td>\n<td>Would you like some coffee?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>request</td>\n<td><em>can, will</em> (informal); <em>could, would</em> (formal)</td>\n<td>Could you pass me the salt, please?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>permission</td>\n<td><em>can</em> (informal); <em>could</em>, <em>may</em> (formal)</td>\n<td>May I take a tea break?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>imaginary situation</td>\n<td><em>would</em></td>\n<td>If I had a lot of money, I would retire early.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>future fact or guess</td>\n<td><em>will</em></td>\n<td>Don’t lend him your car. He’ll crash it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>past habit</td>\n<td><em>would</em> (action verbs); <em>used to</em> (state verbs)</td>\n<td>When I was young, I used to live in an old house.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>deduction</td>\n<td><em>must</em></td>\n<td>It must be power failure.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>obligation</td>\n<td><em>must</em>; <em>shall</em> (legal, strong); <em>should</em> (weak, advice)</td>\n<td>You should inform me before you leave.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>denial</td>\n<td><em>why</em> + <em>should</em></td>\n<td>Why should I do what everyone else does?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>probability</td>\n<td><em>should</em></td>\n<td>This work should take two or three days.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>concession</td>\n<td><em>may / might …, but …</em></td>\n<td>He may be a child, but he is very wise.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"check-yourself\">Check yourself</h2>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Choose the modal: <em>If I won the lottery, I ____ buy a house.</em> (my sentence)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>would.</strong> <em>If I won</em> (if + past simple) makes it a <strong>second</strong> conditional: an unlikely, imagined situation. So the result takes <em>would</em> + base verb. ✗ <em>I will buy a house.</em></p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Correct the sentence: <em>When I was in Class 5, I would live with my grandparents.</em> (my sentence)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><em>live</em> is a <strong>state verb</strong>, and <em>would</em> for past habits goes only with action verbs. ✓ <em>When I was in Class 5, I used to live with my grandparents.</em></p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which reading does <em>must</em> have in <em>The lights are off. They must be asleep</em>? (my sentence)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Necessity</strong>, from a deduction. The speaker sees that the lights are off and concludes something that seems necessary, like <em>The fan is not working. It must be power failure.</em> Nobody is given a duty, so it is not an obligation.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Fill the blank: <em>____ you mind if I opened the window?</em> (my sentence)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Would.</strong> <em>Would you mind</em> is a fixed phrase. After <em>Would you mind if …</em>, the verb goes in the past (<em>opened</em>), as in <em>Would you mind if I borrowed your bike for a couple of days?</em></p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\"><em>We may visit Mexico this summer</em> or <em>We might visit Mexico this summer</em>: which is more likely to happen? Is <em>might</em> a past tense here?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><em>may</em> is more likely: the 5.1 slide calls it a “good probability”. <em>might</em> is more <strong>remote</strong>: a weaker, more uncertain possibility. Neither is past: both talk about this summer. The difference is remoteness, not tense.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Does <em>Why should I see the movie if I don’t like it?</em> ask for reasons?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>No.</strong> <em>Why</em> + <em>should</em> <strong>denies</strong> the relevance of a social requirement. It is a strong refusal, with almost no chance that the speaker will see the movie.</p>\n</div></details>\n"});
